1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,173
This is MATH 239, video 11. The topics are subsets with restrictions, and the proof of inclusion exclusion.

2
00:00:09,623 --> 00:00:16,621
After this video, you can do the four things in this list. The third one is assignment 3, question 1.

3
00:00:17,071 --> 00:00:27,033
Here is the problem of Example 2.24. r sub n is the number of subsets of 1 to n with no two consecutive numbers.

4
00:00:27,483 --> 00:00:33,926
On the left are all 16 subsets of 1 to 4. A filled cell is an element of the subset.

5
00:00:34,376 --> 00:00:40,307
Mark the subsets that contain two consecutive numbers, such as 2 and 3. They are red.

6
00:00:40,757 --> 00:00:46,282
8 subsets are bad. So r sub 4 is 16 minus 8, which is 8.

7
00:00:46,732 --> 00:00:51,212
We want the generating series R of x, as a rational function.

8
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:58,809
The tool of this section changes each subset into a composition. Then the lemmas for compositions apply.

9
00:00:59,259 --> 00:01:04,123
The set U contains the pairs n, A, where A is a subset of 1 to n.

10
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:14,023
We keep n in the pair, because the same set A is a subset of many sets 1 to n. Our example is 7 and the set 2, 3, 6.

11
00:01:14,473 --> 00:01:21,364
Draw n plus 1 cells in a row. Here n is 7, so there are 7 plus 1, which is 8 cells.

12
00:01:21,814 --> 00:01:25,803
Between the cells are 7 boundaries, numbered 1 to 7.

13
00:01:26,253 --> 00:01:31,224
The set A tells us where to cut. A contains 2, so cut at boundary 2.

14
00:01:31,674 --> 00:01:35,087
A also contains 3 and 6. Cut there too.

15
00:01:35,537 --> 00:01:41,425
The cuts make 4 parts: 2, 1, 3, 2. This is the composition of the pair.

16
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:50,238
In the notes, c sub i equals a sub i minus a sub i minus 1, with the end points 0 and n plus 1.

17
00:01:50,688 --> 00:01:55,915
So gamma is 2, 1, 3, 2. It is a composition, and it is not empty.

18
00:01:56,365 --> 00:02:10,210
Now the proof. Put the points a sub 0 to a sub 4 on a number line. a 0 is 0. a 1, a 2 and a 3 are the elements 2, 3 and 6. a 4 is n plus 1, which is 8.

19
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:15,865
The points increase strictly. So each gap c sub i is a positive integer.

20
00:02:16,315 --> 00:02:20,539
The first gap goes from 0 to 2. So c sub 1 is 2.

21
00:02:20,989 --> 00:02:23,336
The other gaps are 1, 3 and 2.

22
00:02:23,786 --> 00:02:30,954
Now add all the gaps. Each middle point is added once and subtracted once, so the sum telescopes.

23
00:02:31,404 --> 00:02:42,561
Only a sub k plus 1 minus a sub 0 stays, which is n plus 1. Here 2 plus 1 plus 3 plus 2 is 8, which is 7 plus 1.

24
00:02:43,011 --> 00:02:53,550
A has k elements, and there are k plus 1 gaps. So the length of gamma is the size of A plus 1. Here A has 3 elements, and gamma has 4 parts.

25
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,544
The length is at least 1, so gamma is not the empty composition.

26
00:02:58,994 --> 00:03:09,767
The table in the notes: n is the size of gamma minus 1, and the size of A is the length of gamma minus 1. Remember the minus 1 in the size.

27
00:03:10,217 --> 00:03:15,700
Now go back. Start with a nonempty composition. Here it is 2, 1, 3, 2.

28
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:21,419
Add the parts from the left. These are the partial sums. The first one is 2.

29
00:03:21,869 --> 00:03:25,347
The next partial sum is 2 plus 1, which is 3.

30
00:03:25,797 --> 00:03:32,751
The next is 3 plus 3, which is 6. The partial sums 2, 3 and 6 are the positions of the cuts.

31
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:40,284
The last partial sum is 6 plus 2, which is 8, the size of gamma. It is not a cut, so we drop it.

32
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:45,662
So A is the set 2, 3, 6, and n is 8 minus 1, which is 7.

33
00:03:46,112 --> 00:03:47,861
We get the start pair back.

34
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:58,231
Each map undoes the other. So by Proposition 1.11, both maps are bijections. The details are Exercise 2.18.

35
00:03:58,681 --> 00:04:07,855
Here is the map for all subsets of 1, 2, 3. So n is 3, and each composition has size 3 plus 1, which is 4.

36
00:04:08,305 --> 00:04:13,254
The empty set has no cut. It gives the composition with one part, 4.

37
00:04:13,704 --> 00:04:20,915
Each set with one element makes one cut. It gives two parts: 1, 3, then 2, 2, then 3, 1.

38
00:04:21,365 --> 00:04:25,439
Each set with two elements makes two cuts, and gives three parts.

39
00:04:25,889 --> 00:04:28,556
The full set gives 1, 1, 1, 1.

40
00:04:29,006 --> 00:04:36,238
These are all 8 compositions of 4, each one time. A set of size k gives k plus 1 parts.

41
00:04:36,688 --> 00:04:45,221
Each pair gives size n plus 1, which is at least 1. So no pair maps to the empty composition. That is why we remove it.

42
00:04:45,671 --> 00:04:50,941
Check the bijection with generating series. Give the pair n, A the weight n.

43
00:04:51,391 --> 00:04:58,324
There are 2 to the n subsets of 1 to n. So the series of U is 1 over 1 minus 2 x.

44
00:04:58,774 --> 00:05:05,601
Nonempty compositions, by size, have the series x over 1 minus 2 x, from video 10.

45
00:05:06,051 --> 00:05:09,933
The second row is the first row moved one place to the right.

46
00:05:10,383 --> 00:05:20,773
So the series of the compositions is x times the series of U, because the size is n plus 1. We use this shift in every problem of this section.

47
00:05:21,223 --> 00:05:31,676
Now use the bijection for Example 2.24. Take n equal to 7 and A equal to 2, 4, 7. The parts are 2, 2, 3, 1.

48
00:05:32,126 --> 00:05:35,582
A middle part is the gap between two elements of A.

49
00:05:36,032 --> 00:05:44,011
Now take A equal to 2, 3, 6. It contains 2 and 3. Their gap is a middle part equal to 1, in red.

50
00:05:44,461 --> 00:05:50,989
Two consecutive elements of A give a middle part equal to 1. The converse is also true.

51
00:05:51,439 --> 00:05:56,111
So the pair is in R exactly when every middle part is at least 2.

52
00:05:56,561 --> 00:06:05,542
The first part is 1 when 1 is in A. The last part is 1 when n is in A, as in the first example. Both are allowed.

53
00:06:05,992 --> 00:06:14,504
Now write the solution in the course form. R sub n is the set of pairs n, A with no two consecutive numbers in A.

54
00:06:14,954 --> 00:06:23,466
R is the union over all n, and the weight of a pair is n. The top row is R of x. We find it on the next slides.

55
00:06:23,916 --> 00:06:28,439
M is the set of compositions that correspond to the pairs in R.

56
00:06:28,889 --> 00:06:38,915
The size of gamma is n plus 1. So M of x equals x times R of x. We find M of x first, and then divide by x.

57
00:06:39,365 --> 00:06:46,171
Split M by the number of parts, l. For l equal to 1, there is one part, of any positive size.

58
00:06:46,621 --> 00:06:52,615
Its series is x plus x squared plus and so on, which is x over 1 minus x.

59
00:06:53,065 --> 00:06:58,612
For l at least 2, there is a first part, l minus 2 middle parts, and a last part.

60
00:06:59,062 --> 00:07:08,171
A middle part is at least 2. Its series is x squared plus x cubed plus and so on, which is x squared over 1 minus x.

61
00:07:08,621 --> 00:07:14,232
By the Product Lemma, the series for l parts is the product of the series of the parts.

62
00:07:14,682 --> 00:07:30,277
Count the powers of x: 1, plus 2 times l minus 2, plus 1, which is 2 l minus 2. There are l factors of 1 minus x. So the product is x to the 2 l minus 2, over 1 minus x to the l.

63
00:07:30,727 --> 00:07:40,071
The lengths are disjoint cases. So by the Sum Lemma, x R of x is the l equal 1 term, plus the sum over l at least 2.

64
00:07:40,521 --> 00:07:52,297
Put j equal to l minus 2, and take out x squared over 1 minus x squared. What is left is a geometric series in x squared over 1 minus x.

65
00:07:52,747 --> 00:08:01,045
The table shows its powers. That series has no constant term, so each column has finitely many nonzero terms.

66
00:08:01,495 --> 00:08:15,426
Sum the geometric series: 1 over 1 minus x squared over 1 minus x. That is 1 minus x, over 1 minus x minus x squared. The sum row of the table shows it.

67
00:08:15,876 --> 00:08:28,015
Bring both terms to the denominator 1 minus x, times 1 minus x minus x squared. The first numerator is x times 1 minus x minus x squared.

68
00:08:28,465 --> 00:08:44,038
The numerators add to x minus x cubed. That is x times 1 minus x, times 1 plus x. Cancel the factor 1 minus x. We get x plus x squared, over 1 minus x minus x squared.

69
00:08:44,488 --> 00:08:54,984
Divide by x. R of x equals 1 plus x, over 1 minus x minus x squared. This is the answer of Example 2.24.

70
00:08:55,434 --> 00:09:03,775
Expand the answer as a power series. The coefficients are 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34.

71
00:09:04,225 --> 00:09:06,700
Direct counts for n up to 7 agree.

72
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:11,097
For n equal to 4 we get 8, as on the first slide.

73
00:09:11,547 --> 00:09:17,819
Each number is the sum of the two numbers before it. For example, 3 plus 5 is 8.

74
00:09:18,269 --> 00:09:27,463
These are the Fibonacci numbers. Chapter 4 shows how a denominator such as 1 minus x minus x squared gives such a rule.

75
00:09:27,913 --> 00:09:34,207
Here is the method for any condition on the gaps. Step 1: write the condition on the set A.

76
00:09:34,657 --> 00:09:37,878
Step 2: change it into a condition on the parts.

77
00:09:38,328 --> 00:09:47,352
A middle part is the gap between two elements. The first part is a sub 1. The last part is n plus 1 minus a sub k.

78
00:09:47,802 --> 00:09:51,109
Step 3: for each length l, use the Product Lemma.

79
00:09:51,559 --> 00:09:54,673
Step 4: add over all lengths with the Sum Lemma.

80
00:09:55,123 --> 00:10:02,569
When only the middle parts have a condition, you always get this form, with P of x the series of one middle part.

81
00:10:03,019 --> 00:10:10,869
Step 5: divide by x, because the size is n plus 1. Then check the first few coefficients by hand.

82
00:10:11,319 --> 00:10:18,978
Now an exercise from the notes. Pause the video and try it. Consecutive elements must differ by at most 2.

83
00:10:19,428 --> 00:10:23,481
The set 1, 3, 4 is allowed: its gaps are 2 and 1.

84
00:10:23,931 --> 00:10:29,393
The set 1, 4 is not allowed, because its gap is 4 minus 1, which is 3.

85
00:10:29,843 --> 00:10:38,589
Consecutive elements give the middle parts. So a middle part is 1 or 2, and its series P of x is x plus x squared.

86
00:10:39,039 --> 00:10:42,410
The first and last parts can be any positive integer.

87
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:45,441
Put P into the formula of the method.

88
00:10:45,891 --> 00:10:49,646
Divide by x, and bring the terms to one denominator.

89
00:10:50,096 --> 00:11:04,048
Expand the numerator: 1 minus x, times 1 minus x minus x squared, is 1 minus 2 x plus x cubed. Add x. The numerator is 1 minus x plus x cubed.

90
00:11:04,498 --> 00:11:08,637
The coefficients are 1, 2, 4, 8, 15, 27.

91
00:11:09,087 --> 00:11:17,791
For n equal to 4, only the set 1, 4 fails. So the count is 16 minus 1, which is 15, and it agrees.

92
00:11:18,241 --> 00:11:24,193
Now assignment 3, question 1. It also uses gaps. Pause the video and try it.

93
00:11:24,643 --> 00:11:37,571
S is the set of triples a, b, c of natural numbers with a at most b at most c. The weight is a plus b plus c. The figure shows the triple 1, 3, 4 as three bars.

94
00:11:38,021 --> 00:11:45,637
Cut each bar at the heights a and b. The bottom piece has height x equal to a. It is in all three bars.

95
00:11:46,087 --> 00:11:54,663
The middle piece has height y equal to b minus a: here 3 minus 1, which is 2. It is in the bars of b and c.

96
00:11:55,113 --> 00:12:03,433
The top piece has height z equal to c minus b: here 4 minus 3, which is 1. It is only in the bar of c.

97
00:12:03,883 --> 00:12:12,011
x, y and z are any natural numbers. x is in 3 bars, y is in 2 bars, and z is in 1 bar.

98
00:12:12,461 --> 00:12:16,834
So a plus b plus c equals 3 x plus 2 y plus z.

99
00:12:17,284 --> 00:12:25,796
For 1, 3, 4: 3 times 1, plus 2 times 2, plus 1 is 8. And 1 plus 3 plus 4 is also 8.

100
00:12:26,246 --> 00:12:33,499
Here is the posted solution. Let T be N cubed: all triples x, y, z of natural numbers.

101
00:12:33,949 --> 00:12:39,133
Give T the weight w prime, equal to 3 x plus 2 y plus z.

102
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:42,933
On the left are the 4 triples in S of weight 4.

103
00:12:43,383 --> 00:12:51,148
g takes a, b, c to a, b minus a, c minus b. For example, 1, 1, 2 goes to 1, 0, 1.

104
00:12:51,598 --> 00:12:57,785
Its inverse f takes partial sums. The question lets you state the inverse without a proof.

105
00:12:58,235 --> 00:13:05,424
g keeps the weight: 3 a, plus 2 times b minus a, plus c minus b, is a plus b plus c.

106
00:13:05,874 --> 00:13:14,450
So g is a bijection between the elements of weight n in S and in T, for every n. So the two series are equal.

107
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:24,905
T is a Cartesian product of three copies of N. The weight is a sum: 3 x, plus 2 y, plus z. So the Product Lemma applies.

108
00:13:25,355 --> 00:13:34,571
N with the weight i times m has the series 1 over 1 minus x to the i. The rows show i equal to 3, 2 and 1.

109
00:13:35,021 --> 00:13:42,360
The product is 1 over 1 minus x, times 1 minus x squared, times 1 minus x cubed.

110
00:13:42,810 --> 00:13:45,775
Check: the coefficient of x to the 4 is 4.

111
00:13:46,225 --> 00:13:50,471
S has 4 triples of weight 4. They are in the green box.

112
00:13:50,921 --> 00:14:00,563
In a written answer, define T and w prime, give the bijection and its inverse, show that it keeps the weight, and name the Product Lemma.

113
00:14:01,013 --> 00:14:11,061
Section 2.5 proves inclusion exclusion from video 5. A sub S is the intersection of the sets A sub i for i in S.

114
00:14:11,511 --> 00:14:20,855
The size of the union is the sum over nonempty S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1, times the size of A sub S.

115
00:14:21,305 --> 00:14:34,255
For three sets, first add the sizes of A 1, A 2 and A 3. The numbers show how often each region is counted. The center is in all three sets, so it is counted 3 times.

116
00:14:34,705 --> 00:14:43,558
Subtract the three pairwise intersections. A pair region is in one of them. So it is now counted 2 minus 1, which is once.

117
00:14:44,008 --> 00:14:51,603
The center is in all three pairwise intersections. So it is counted 3 minus 3, which is zero times.

118
00:14:52,053 --> 00:14:58,047
Add the triple intersection. The center is now counted 0 plus 1, which is once.

119
00:14:58,497 --> 00:15:03,873
Now every region is counted exactly once. The proof shows this for any m.

120
00:15:04,323 --> 00:15:14,713
The proof needs one lemma. For a nonempty set T, the sum over the nonempty subsets S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1 is 1.

121
00:15:15,163 --> 00:15:19,408
The figure shows the 8 subsets of T equal to 1, 2, 3.

122
00:15:19,858 --> 00:15:28,797
From the proof of the binomial theorem, the sum over all subsets S of x to the size of S is 1 plus x, to the n.

123
00:15:29,247 --> 00:15:39,231
Both sides are polynomials, so put x equal to minus 1. The right side is 0 to the n, which is 0 because n is at least 1.

124
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:43,691
In the figure: 1 minus 3 plus 3 minus 1 is 0.

125
00:15:44,141 --> 00:15:48,024
The empty set gives the term 1. Move it out of the sum.

126
00:15:48,474 --> 00:15:53,637
Multiply by minus 1. The sum over the nonempty subsets is 1.

127
00:15:54,087 --> 00:15:57,223
In the figure: 3 minus 3 plus 1 is 1.

128
00:15:57,673 --> 00:16:04,201
Now the proof. Let V be the union. We find how much one element v adds to the right side.

129
00:16:04,651 --> 00:16:12,779
Let T of v be the set of indices i with v in A sub i. v is in the union, so T of v is not empty.

130
00:16:13,229 --> 00:16:18,541
In the figure, v is in A 1 and A 3 only. So T of v is 1, 3.

131
00:16:18,991 --> 00:16:28,441
v is in A sub S exactly when v is in every A sub i with i in S. That means S is a nonempty subset of T of v.

132
00:16:28,891 --> 00:16:38,747
So v is counted in A 1, A 3, and A 1 3, with the signs plus, plus, minus. The total is 1 plus 1 minus 1, which is 1.

133
00:16:39,197 --> 00:16:44,403
In general, v adds the sum of Lemma 2.25, which is 1.

134
00:16:44,853 --> 00:16:48,778
Here is the whole proof in one table, for three small sets.

135
00:16:49,228 --> 00:17:04,972
Each row is an element v. Each column is a nonempty set S. A cell has the sign of S when v is in A sub S. For example, 3 is in A 1 and A 2, so row 3 has minus 1 in column 1 2.

136
00:17:05,422 --> 00:17:13,443
The right side of the theorem adds the table column by column. Each column gives the size of A sub S with its sign.

137
00:17:13,893 --> 00:17:20,891
The columns add to 4 plus 3 plus 4 minus 2 minus 1 minus 2 plus 1, which is 7.

138
00:17:21,341 --> 00:17:29,533
Now add the same table row by row. A row contains the sets S with S a nonempty subset of T of v.

139
00:17:29,983 --> 00:17:44,148
By Lemma 2.25, each row adds to 1. For example, 4 is in all three sets. Its row is 1 plus 1 plus 1 minus 1 minus 1 minus 1 plus 1, which is 1.

140
00:17:44,598 --> 00:17:52,683
So the total is the number of rows: the size of V, which is 7. Both ways give 7. This completes the proof.

141
00:17:53,133 --> 00:18:05,741
Example 2.27 uses inclusion exclusion. Euler's function phi of n is the number of b from 1 to n with gcd of b and n equal to 1.

142
00:18:06,191 --> 00:18:15,023
Take n equal to 12, which is 2 squared times 3. b shares a factor with 12 exactly when 2 or 3 divides b.

143
00:18:15,473 --> 00:18:19,399
A 1 is the set of multiples of 2. There are 6 of them.

144
00:18:19,849 --> 00:18:24,051
A 2 is the set of multiples of 3. There are 4 of them.

145
00:18:24,501 --> 00:18:30,795
A 1 intersect A 2 is the set of multiples of 6. There are 2 of them: 6 and 12.

146
00:18:31,245 --> 00:18:37,111
By inclusion exclusion, the union has 6 plus 4 minus 2, which is 8 elements.

147
00:18:37,561 --> 00:18:46,735
The other numbers are coprime to 12. Phi of 12 is 12 minus 8, which is 4. They are 1, 5, 7 and 11.

148
00:18:47,185 --> 00:18:57,787
Now any n, with different primes p 1 to p m. b is in A sub S exactly when the product of the p sub i, for i in S, divides b.

149
00:18:58,237 --> 00:19:01,672
So the size of A sub S is n over that product.

150
00:19:02,122 --> 00:19:07,647
Phi of n is n minus the size of the union. Put in inclusion exclusion.

151
00:19:08,097 --> 00:19:11,895
Move n into the sum, as the term for the empty set.

152
00:19:12,345 --> 00:19:20,259
On the right, expand a product of two factors. Each term picks 1 or minus 1 over p i from each factor.

153
00:19:20,709 --> 00:19:31,589
S is the set of factors where we pick minus 1 over p i. For example, the term 1 times minus 1 over p 2 has S equal to the set 2.

154
00:19:32,039 --> 00:19:40,082
So the sum is the expanded product. Phi of n equals n times the product of 1 minus 1 over p i.

155
00:19:40,532 --> 00:19:44,671
For 12: 12 times one half times two thirds is 4.

156
00:19:45,121 --> 00:19:48,918
Try one more. Pause the video and find phi of 60.

157
00:19:49,368 --> 00:19:52,355
60 is 2 squared times 3 times 5.

158
00:19:52,805 --> 00:20:01,274
The product formula gives 60 times 1 minus one half, times 1 minus one third, times 1 minus one fifth.

159
00:20:01,724 --> 00:20:07,335
That is 60 times one half times two thirds times four fifths, which is 16.

160
00:20:07,785 --> 00:20:13,694
The green cells are the 16 numbers from 1 to 60 that are coprime to 60.

161
00:20:14,144 --> 00:20:22,101
Inclusion exclusion gives the same number. Subtract the multiples of 2, 3 and 5: 30, 20 and 12.

162
00:20:22,551 --> 00:20:27,330
Add back the multiples of 6, 10 and 15: 10, 6 and 4.

163
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:30,532
Subtract the multiples of 30: 2.

164
00:20:30,982 --> 00:20:36,379
60 minus 62, plus 20, minus 2 is 16, the same number.

165
00:20:36,829 --> 00:20:44,253
To recap. The parts are the gaps, with end points 0 and n plus 1. The inverse takes partial sums.

166
00:20:44,703 --> 00:20:49,802
The size is n plus 1, so M of x equals x times R of x.

167
00:20:50,252 --> 00:20:58,679
A gap condition is a condition on the middle parts. Use the Product Lemma for each length, and the Sum Lemma over all lengths.

168
00:20:59,129 --> 00:21:06,659
With no two consecutive numbers, R of x is 1 plus x, over 1 minus x minus x squared.

169
00:21:07,109 --> 00:21:13,595
For assignment 3 question 1, a bijection that keeps the weight moves the problem to N cubed.

170
00:21:14,045 --> 00:21:20,210
In inclusion exclusion, each element adds exactly 1, by Lemma 2.25.

171
00:21:20,660 --> 00:21:30,217
This gives phi of n as n times the product of 1 minus 1 over p i. The next video starts chapter 3, binary strings.
